**Theorem 29.1 (a paid common tuple upper).** For every $D\ge1$ and
nonempty public $Q\subseteq\mathcal P_D$,

$$
M_D(Q)\le\prod_{p\in Q}m(|p|).
\tag{GDB.113}
$$

One preparation uses exactly $2^D-1$ original-root Reads, maximum
address length $D$ and exactly $(D-1)2^D+1$ submitted letters on
every source. Every later request uses one actual prefix Read of
length at most $D$, with the original all-authentic-count guarantee.

Proof. Install a fixed public order of $Q$ and all its grouping tables.
On the one stationary $t$, before any request, submit every $Rw$,
$|w|\le D-1$, in a fixed public order. All the prefixes of every
$p\in Q$ occur in this same acquired array. Compute from it

$$
E_p(t)=g_{|p|}(\partial_p(t)),\qquad
J_Q(t)=(E_p(t))_{p\in Q}.
\tag{GDB.114}
$$

Export only this fixed-slot complete tuple and isolate the acquired
array, descriptors and other preparation dependence as in Theorem
27.2. Its actual attained cardinality obeys

$$
|J_Q[\mathcal F_D]|\le\prod_{p\in Q}|E_p[\mathcal F_D]|
                         =\prod_{p\in Q}m(|p|).
\tag{GDB.115}
$$

The left side is one joint image on the same source. Equality with the
product is neither needed nor asserted; component names may be
arbitrarily correlated. No separately chosen sources are joined to
make the tuple. On receiving $p$, choose its public slot, and apply
Theorem 27.2's single center-prefix Read to that same stationary $t$.
The slot value and its reply give $\Gamma_p(t)$ before any supplied
count, so (GDB.4) completes the required prediction.

The scan words have lengths $j$ with multiplicity $2^{j-1}$, for
$j=1,\ldots,D$. Hence the exact charges are

$$
\sum_{j=1}^D2^{j-1}=2^D-1,\qquad
\sum_{j=1}^Dj2^{j-1}=(D-1)2^D+1.
\tag{GDB.116}
$$

This is the credited [FMR, Theorem 2.3][FMR]/[CBN, Theorem 7.1][CBN]
canonical acquisition scan, used here simply to obtain the actual
prefix replies. It does not require acquiring deeper source structure.
Every request and every original shape is covered; no actual cut or
shape descriptor is installed for free. $\square$

